A Formal Architecture for Safety-Preserving Authorization Under Imperfect and Correlated Observability

A Theory of Sequential Payload Deployment from Reusable Launch Vehicles

Final Version (v6.0) — Consolidated After Seven Adversarial Rounds

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

Affiliation: Independent Research

Status: Pre-print, not peer-reviewed, released as a falsification target

Disclaimer: No claim of flight readiness. This is a formal research architecture bearing the enumerated open problems as its falsification surface.

Abstract

We present v6.0 of a formal architecture for sequential payload deployment from reusable launch vehicles, instanced on the SpaceX Starship "Pez Dispenser." The central thesis is that deployment authorization is not a mechanical event but an epistemic decision under imperfect, correlated, and state-dependent observability, whose authority must not be inherited across payloads. The v6.0 revision responds to a seventh adversarial round that identified (i) a residual error in the PCR6 multi-source summation condition, (ii) a logically circular non-emptiness argument for the copula-restricted credal set, (iii) the absence of a compatibility condition for the six-source joint copula model, (iv) a HOLD deadlock in the TLA⁺ kernel, (v) invariants claimed but not implemented, (vi) an undefined probability-of-unsafe-deployment object, (vii) a missing physical-to-abstract refinement relation, and (viii) an incorrectly scoped aggregate collision obligation. We correct each, and we advance the architecture by (a) replacing the tautological copula non-emptiness proposition with an attainability condition, (b) introducing authorization epochs as the formal device for non-inheritance, (c) reformulating the central safety condition as a credal-set supremum rather than a probability, (d) adding a HOLD exit action with re-evaluation semantics, and (e) defining the aggregate collision obligation as a union-bound surrogate. After seven rounds, four propositions stand: sequential authorization with per-payload reset is a legitimate safety problem distinct from single-shot separation; conflict and dependence are distinct mathematical phenomena whose conflation is a documented error; an adversarial environment action is the correct discipline for non-vacuous TLA⁺ invariants; and a discrete authorization kernel admits a TLA⁺ specification with the evidential layer abstracted, provided the abstraction relation is explicit. Everything else is offered as a candidate for further development only after the logged repairs.

Keywords: payload deployment, evidence theory, DSmT, PCR6, formal verification, TLA⁺, credal sets, copulas, signed dependence, safety kernel, correlated observability, reusable launch vehicles, authorization epochs.

Chapter 1 — Introduction

1.1 Motivation

The deployment of payloads from reusable launch vehicles is a safety-critical operation whose failure modes extend beyond mission loss. An unauthorized or premature deployment can generate orbital debris, create conjunction hazards, and incur regulatory liability. The Starship "Pez Dispenser" has changed the character of the problem: deployment is no longer a single separation event but a sequential process during which the vehicle's mass, inertia tensor, thermal state, and collision-risk profile mutate continuously.

The naive formulation — "how to place a satellite in the bay and release it" — reduces deployment to a mechanical liberation. The real difficulty is epistemic: given imperfect observations, sources that may share common-mode error, and a plant that changes between decisions, when is liberation authorized?

1.2 Problem Statement

Problem P. Given an RLV in a known orbital state, $N$ payloads in a shared bay, and observations $O(t)$, determine a sequence $A_1, \ldots, A_N \in \mathcal{A}$ such that:

1. the probability of unsafe deployment is bounded;
2. each decision is independently auditable;
3. the authorization for payload $i+1$ is not inherited from that for payload $i$.

Condition (3) is the dissertation's central commitment.

1.3 Epistemic Policy

Every external factual claim is structured as $\text{Claim} \to \text{Primary source} \to \text{Observation} \to \text{Inference}$. Secondary news reports are marked secondary-consistent. The ledger distinguishes primary-confirmed, secondary-consistent, and projected-at-time-of-writing.

1.4 Thesis Statement

Sequential payload deployment from reusable launch vehicles can be represented as a safety-preserving state-transition problem whose central object is an epistemic authorization decision, and this representation admits a formally specified discrete authorization kernel — with the evidential layer abstracted — provided that authorization is reset per payload, that statistical dependence is distinguished from evidential conflict, that the decision rule is evaluated worst-case over a credal set, and that the physical-to-abstract refinement relation is stated explicitly.

The thesis does not claim completeness, correctness of thresholds, flight-readiness, or that the evidential layer is verified.

Chapter 2 — Background

2.1 Separation as a Hazard Class

Guernsey et al. (AAS 03-071) report that from 1983 to 2005, spacecraft and fairing separation systems accounted for approximately 10% of launch failures.

2.2 Redundancy and Common-Cause Failure

NASA HR-31 requires at least single failure tolerance to catastrophic events, with the caveat that "redundancy alone does not meet the intent" and that dissimilar redundancy "can be very effective provided there is sufficient separation among the redundant legs." The most insidious failure mode is software common-mode failure: "every instance of the same software image executing on multiple devices encounters the same unexpected condition simultaneously."

2.3 Formal Methods in Space Systems

NASA's Gateway VSM employs assume-guarantee contracts in PlusCal/TLA⁺, with contracts as temporal formulas evaluated by TLC. DLR has demonstrated symbolic model checking to 250 state machines. This dissertation adopts the same discipline: the DSK specification is written in TLA⁺, with contracts scoping the evidential layer, and with an adversarial Environment action to ensure that invariants are non-vacuous.

2.4 Evidence Theory, Conflict, and Dependence

DST requires independence. Two orthogonal pathologies are distinguished in the literature:

· Conflict: mutually inconsistent evidence, handled by PCR5/PCR6.
· Dependence: statistically dependent sources, handled by random-set theory, copulas, or β-factor decomposition.

PCR5 and PCR6 are not associative. The sequential combination of $S > 2$ BBAs is not in general equal to the global combination. PCR6 is preferred over PCR5 for $S > 2$.

2.5 Random Sets and Copulas

Random sets induce belief and plausibility functions. Copulas describe dependence between random sets. A joint belief function is related to its marginals by a family of copulas; a single copula is generally insufficient.

2.6 Gap

No reviewed framework jointly addresses (a) sequential deployment, (b) evidence-theoretic authorization under correlated observability with explicit conflict/dependence separation, (c) formal verification of non-inherited authorization, and (d) constellation-aware state mutation.

