6.6 Authorization Epoch and Non-Inheritance

The central non-inheritance commitment is formalized by an authorization epoch:

$e_i = (i, \nu_i, t_i, \mathcal{E}_i, \text{model}_i)$

where $i$ is the payload identifier, $\nu_i$ is the authorization epoch counter, $t_i$ is the authorization timestamp, $\mathcal{E}_i$ is the evidence snapshot, and $\text{model}_i$ is the decision-kernel version.

$Authorize_i = \operatorname{Decision}(O_i, \Sigma_i, e_i)$.

$Deploy_i \Rightarrow A_i = \text{AUTHORIZED} \land \text{epoch}(A_i) = \text{epoch}_i \land \text{payload}(A_i) = i \land \text{fresh}(e_i)$.

The central non-inheritance invariant is

$\boxed{Deploy_i \Rightarrow \text{epoch}(A_i) = \text{epoch}_i}$

and

$\boxed{\text{epoch}_i \neq \text{epoch}_{i-1}} \quad \text{for every } i > 1$.

This is stronger than resetting $A$ to UNAUTHORIZED; it binds the authorization token to a particular payload, evidence snapshot, and kernel version.

6.7 Physical-to-Abstract Refinement Relation

The formal kernel operates on an abstract state $s_{\text{abs}}$. The physical system occupies $s_{\text{phys}}$. A refinement relation $R(s_{\text{phys}}, s_{\text{abs}})$ must hold for the kernel's safety claims to transfer.

$R(s_{\text{phys}}, P_{\text{abs}} = \text{CAPTIVE})$ holds only if the physical payload is mechanically restrained, in no free-flight state, and capture is verified.

$R(s_{\text{phys}}, P_{\text{abs}} = \text{SEPARATED})$ holds only if the physical payload has achieved separation velocity, is outside the bay envelope, and no re-contact is possible.

$R(s_{\text{phys}}, P_{\text{abs}} = \text{UNCERTAIN})$ holds when the physical state is unknown or in transition.

Without $R$, a proof of the kernel's safety invariant is a proof about a fictional system. The full conditional theorem is

$A_{\text{physical}} \land A_{\text{evidence}} \land R_{\text{abstraction}} \land \text{Spec}_{\text{DSK}} \Rightarrow \text{SafetyInvariant}$.

6.8 Scalability

For $N = 26$, the discrete state space is dominated by $t_{\text{hold}}$, giving $240 \cdot (t_{\text{hold}}^{\text{max}} + 1)^{N+1}$ combinations. With $t_{\text{hold}}^{\text{max}} = 10$: approximately $3.15 \times 10^{30}$. Assume-guarantee decomposition is adopted: phase-specific contracts are verified in isolation before composition.

6.9 Honest Limitations

The module abstracts $Verdict$, $ProjectedPc_x1e6$, and the physical collision semantics as nondeterministically driven variables. It verifies the discrete control logic — authorization grant, hold, re-evaluation, inhibit, abort, reset, terminalize, fault-clear — but does not verify the evidential layer or the physical layer. The refinement relation $R$ is stated but not proved. The claim of state-dependent authorization in the kernel is retracted; state-dependence lives in the layer interfaces.

