9.1 What This Dissertation Claims

That sequential deployment can be represented as a safety-preserving state-transition problem whose central object is an epistemic authorization decision; that this representation admits a formally specified discrete authorization kernel — with the evidential layer abstracted and the refinement relation stated — with per-payload reset, authorization epochs, per-payload hold accounting, a HOLD re-evaluation path, exclusive terminal phase, projected aggregate collision obligation as a union-bound surrogate, and an adversarial environment capable of forcing phase exit; and that the conflict/dependence distinction is the correct formal apparatus, with the multi-source extension conditional on OP17.

