Appendix B — Frame of Discernment: Formal Development

B.1 The Four-Atom Frame

Let $\Theta = \{\text{SAFE}, \text{MECH}, \text{THERM}, \text{DEB}\}$. The hazard set is $\text{HAZ} = \{\text{MECH}, \text{THERM}, \text{DEB}\}$.

B.2 DST Reading

Under DST, $\Theta$ is a partition: atoms are mutually exclusive and exhaustive. Every subset $A \subseteq \Theta$ corresponds to a proposition, and mass assignments satisfy

$m: 2^\Theta \to [0,1], \qquad \sum_{A \subseteq \Theta} m(A) = 1, \qquad m(\varnothing) = 0$.

Belief and plausibility are

$\text{Bel}(A) = \sum_{B \subseteq A} m(B), \qquad \text{Pl}(A) = \sum_{B \cap A \neq \varnothing} m(B)$.

The duality $\text{Pl}(A) = 1 - \text{Bel}(\bar{A})$ holds identically. With $\bar{\text{SAFE}} = \text{HAZ}$:

$\text{Pl}(\text{HAZ}) = 1 - \text{Bel}(\text{SAFE})$. (B.1)

B.3 DSmT Free-Model Reading

Under DSmT, exclusivity is dropped. Mass is assigned over the hyper-power set $G^\Theta$, which is closed under union and intersection but not complement. For $|\Theta| = 4$, $|G^\Theta|$ is the Dedekind number $D_4 = 168$.

Mass on intersections (e.g., $m(\text{SAFE} \cap \text{MECH})$ or $m(\text{MECH} \cap \text{THERM} \cap \text{DEB})$) is permitted. The duality (B.1) fails:

$\text{Pl}(\text{HAZ}) \neq 1 - \text{Bel}(\text{SAFE})$ in general. (B.2)

This is because $\text{Pl}(\text{HAZ})$ sums mass over all sets intersecting HAZ, while $1 - \text{Bel}(\text{SAFE})$ sums mass over all sets not contained in SAFE. A set like $\text{SAFE} \cap \text{MECH}$ intersects HAZ but is not contained in SAFE, so it contributes to $\text{Pl}(\text{HAZ})$ but not to $1 - \text{Bel}(\text{SAFE})$, creating the discrepancy.

B.4 Ignorance Representation

Ignorance is mass on $\Theta$ itself, not on a distinguished atom. This is the correct representation under both readings. A distinguished atom IGN would be a fifth proposition; ignorance of which of the four atoms holds is a mass on their union, i.e., $m(\Theta)$.

B.5 Abstraction from Physical Latent State

Let $\mathcal{Z}$ be a physical latent state space with elements including

$z_{\text{att}}$ (attitude-control failure),
$z_{\text{pow}}$ (power failure),
$z_{\text{cmd}}$ (command corruption),
$z_{\text{door}}$ (door-state uncertainty),
$z_{\text{nav}}$ (navigation uncertainty),
$z_{\text{coll}}$ (collision with launch vehicle),
$z_{\text{cmf}}$ (sensor common-mode failure).

The authorization abstraction is a map $h: \mathcal{Z} \to \Theta$ defined by

$h(z) = \begin{cases}
\text{SAFE} & \text{if } z \text{ is nominal across all subsystems} \\
\text{MECH} & \text{if } z \in \{z_{\text{att}}, z_{\text{door}}, z_{\text{coll}}\} \\
\text{THERM} & \text{if } z \text{ is a thermal fault} \\
\text{DEB} & \text{if } z \text{ is a debris-environment fault} \\
\Theta & \text{otherwise (attribution incomplete)}
\end{cases}$

The map $h$ is not injective: multiple physical states map to the same abstract atom. The evidential machinery estimates beliefs about $\Theta$, not $\mathcal{Z}$. The information lost by $h$ is one source of abstraction error, subsumed in the refinement relation $R$.

