Appendix C — Evidence Theory Foundations

C.1 Belief, Plausibility, Commonality

For BBA $m$ on $\Theta$:

$\text{Bel}(A) = \sum_{B \subseteq A} m(B)$,
$\text{Pl}(A) = \sum_{B \cap A \neq \varnothing} m(B)$,
$Q(A) = \sum_{B \supseteq A} m(B)$ (commonality).

The three are related by Möbius inversion:

$m(A) = \sum_{B \subseteq A} (-1)^{|A \setminus B|} \text{Bel}(B)$,
$\text{Pl}(A) = \sum_{B \subseteq A} (-1)^{|B|} Q(B)$.

C.2 Dempster's Rule

For independent sources with BBAs $m_1, m_2$:

$(m_1 \oplus m_2)(A) = \frac{1}{1-K} \sum_{B \cap C = A} m_1(B) m_2(C)$, (C.1)

with conflict

$K = \sum_{B \cap C = \varnothing} m_1(B) m_2(C)$. (C.2)

If $K = 1$, the combination is undefined.

C.3 Zadeh's Paradox

For $m_1(\{a\}) = 0.99, m_1(\{b\}) = 0.01$, $m_2(\{c\}) = 0.99, m_2(\{c\}) = ...$ — actually with $m_2(\{b\}) = 0.01, m_2(\{c\}) = 0.99$ and $\{a\}, \{b\}, \{c\}$ disjoint:

$K = 0.99 \cdot 0.99 = 0.9801$,
$(m_1 \oplus m_2)(\{b\}) = \frac{0.01 \cdot 0.01}{1 - 0.9801} = 0.005$,
$(m_1 \oplus m_2)(\{c\}) = \frac{0.99 \cdot 0.99}{1 - 0.9801} = 0.995$.

Dempster's rule assigns 0.995 to $\{c\}$, which was supported by only one source with mass 0.99 and contradicted the other. This is Zadeh's paradox.

C.4 PCR5 (Two Sources)

PCR5 redistributes the conflicting mass back to the sources that produced it, proportionally to their masses:

$m_{\text{PCR5}}(X) = m_\cap(X) + \sum_{\substack{Y \cap Z = \varnothing \\ Y \cup Z = X}} \left[ \frac{m_1(Y)^2 m_2(Z)}{m_1(Y) + m_2(Z)} + \frac{m_2(Z)^2 m_1(Y)}{m_2(Z) + m_1(Y)} \right]$. (C.3)

C.5 PCR6 (General $S$ Sources)

For $S \geq 2$ sources, the standard PCR6 formula (Martin & Osswald) is

$m_{\text{PCR6}}(X) = m_\cap(X) + \sum_{i=1}^{S} \sum_{\substack{Y_j \in G^\Theta, \; j \neq i \\ X \cap \bigcap_{j \neq i} Y_j = \varnothing}} \frac{m_i(X)^2 \prod_{j \neq i} m_j(Y_j)}{m_i(X) + \sum_{j \neq i} m_j(Y_j)}$. (C.4)

The outer sum is over sources $i$; the inner sum is over focal element tuples $(Y_j)_{j \neq i}$ such that $X$ is disjoint from the intersection of the other focal elements. The mass is redistributed to $X$ from the conflicting configuration.

C.6 Comparison of PCR5 and PCR6

For $S = 2$, PCR5 and PCR6 coincide: the intersection of the singleton set $\{Y_j : j \neq i\}$ is just $Y_j$ itself, and the union condition also reduces to $X \cap Y_j = \varnothing$.

For $S > 2$, they diverge. PCR6 is preferred because it preserves the associativity structure of the combination better under iteration and avoids over-redistribution to combinatorial intersections.

C.7 Non-Associativity

Neither PCR5 nor PCR6 is associative:

$((m_1 \star m_2) \star m_3) \neq (m_1 \star (m_2 \star m_3))$ in general.

Consequently, sequential pairwise combination of $S > 2$ BBAs does not equal global combination. For Layer C with $S = 6$, PCR6 is applied globally, not sequentially.

C.8 Vacuous BBA Non-Neutrality

The vacuous BBA $m_\Theta(\Theta) = 1$ is neutral under Dempster's rule: $m \oplus m_\Theta = m$. Under PCR6 for $S > 2$, it is not neutral: the vacuous source contributes to the conflict terms and distorts redistribution. Sources with $m_i(\Theta) > 1 - \varepsilon$ are therefore gated out before fusion.

