Appendix I — Refinement Relation: Formal Development

I.1 Physical State Space

The physical state space $S_{\text{phys}}$ includes continuous variables (position, velocity, attitude, temperature, stress, deformation, etc.) and discrete modes (door open/closed, clamp engaged/released, etc.). A full specification is beyond this document.

I.2 Abstract State Space

The abstract state $s_{\text{abs}}$ is an element of $\mathcal{Q} = \mathcal{P} \times \mathcal{D} \times \mathcal{F} \times \mathcal{A} \times \mathcal{E} \times \mathcal{I}$.

I.3 Refinement Relation

$R \subseteq S_{\text{phys}} \times \mathcal{Q}$ is a relation such that for each $q \in \mathcal{Q}$, the set $\{s \in S_{\text{phys}} : R(s, q)\}$ is non-empty.

I.4 Conditions for Specific Abstract States

CAPTIVE. $R(s, \text{CAPTIVE})$ iff the payload is mechanically restrained by the dispenser; the door is closed or the payload is fully inside the bay envelope; separation velocity is zero; and no free-flight state applies.

SEPARATING. $R(s, \text{SEPARATING})$ iff the release mechanism is actuated, the payload has non-zero relative velocity but has not cleared the bay envelope, and no re-contact is possible within the predicted trajectory.

SEPARATED. $R(s, \text{SEPARATED})$ iff the payload has achieved the specified separation velocity, is outside the bay envelope, and no re-contact with the vehicle or other payloads is predicted within a specified horizon.

UNCERTAIN. $R(s, \text{UNCERTAIN})$ iff the physical state cannot be classified into any of the above with the available sensor evidence.

I.5 Soundness

The kernel proves invariants over $\mathcal{Q}$. A physical invariant $I_{\text{phys}}$ is implied by a kernel invariant $I_{\text{abs}}$ if

$I_{\text{abs}}(q) \land R(s, q) \Rightarrow I_{\text{phys}}(s)$. (I.1)

This implication is not established in this document. It is a proof obligation registered as OP19.

I.6 The Classic Trap

Without $R$, the kernel's correctness is correctness about a fictional system. The invariant Inv_NoCaptive may be proven, but this proves nothing about the physical payload unless CAPTIVE correctly captures mechanical restraint and the physical system is known to satisfy $R$.

The treatment of P' = "CAPTIVE" after abort is the critical point: the abstract state asserts capture, but physical capture may not be established. The refinement relation must therefore include a recovery condition: $R(s, \text{CAPTIVE})$ holds after abort only if a re-capture operation has physically restored the payload to a restrained state.

