Appendix N — Falsification Protocol: Detailed Rules

N.1 No Silent Repair

Every correction must be logged in the attack matrix with:

· Original claim.
· Counterexample.
· Severity classification.
· Required revision.
· Version of correction.

N.2 Counterexample Format

$(\text{Claim}, \text{Counterexample}, \text{Severity}, \text{Required Revision})$. (N.1)

N.3 Severity Classes

· Critical: invalidates a core theorem or breaks soundness of the formal kernel.
· Major: affects a significant claim but not the core architecture.
· Minor: cosmetic, nomenclature, or proof-detail issue.

N.4 Independent Review

No system adjudicates its own critique. Each adversarial round is conducted by a system distinct from the one that produced the version under review.

N.5 Adversarial Round Protocol

1. Present the current version as the target.
2. Enumerate all claims subject to attack.
3. For each claim, produce a counterexample or affirm support.
4. Classify severity.
5. Produce a required revision.
6. Log in the attack matrix.
7. Only after logging may the revision be applied.

N.6 Retraction Protocol

A claim that cannot be supported is retracted, not weakened into ambiguity. The retraction is logged in the attack matrix and the claim removed from the defensible core.

N.7 Open Problem Protocol

An unresolved issue that is not a contradiction is registered as an open problem with:

· Identifier (OPn).
· Statement.
· Blocking status (whether it blocks a downstream claim).
· Interaction with other open problems.

